<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · mountain</p><h1 class="enc-title">Mount Taygetus</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Mount <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/taygetus">Taygetus</a> is a mountain range in the southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peloponnese">Peloponnese</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greece">Greece</a>, rising to about 2,400 metres and separating <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/laconia">Laconia</a> from Messenia. Ancient <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sparta">Sparta</a> lay at its eastern foot, and the Spartan conquest of Messenia across the range shaped the city's history. In the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/middle-ages">Middle Ages</a> it was home to the Slavic <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/melingoi">Melingoi</a>, and the Byzantine town of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mystras">Mystras</a> grew on one of its spurs.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/mount-taygetus.jpg" alt="Mount Taygetus" width="320" height="213" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Herbert Ortner, Vienna, Austria · CC BY 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Taygetos_Ilias_1.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
